  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 233 PM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nA broad ridging pattern persists through the work week before \ntroughing off the southern California coast begins to move \nashore by Thursday morning. Widespread highs in the 70s are in\nstore through Friday with record warm overnight temperatures in\nthe 50s likely Friday. There is now high confidence that a \nprogressive trough ejects into the Northern Plains by Friday \nnight. This and the associated cold front behind it support a \nwindy and much cooler (albeit seasonable) pattern change with \nlimited rainfall chances. A stray thunderstorm cannot be ruled \nout Friday given the unusual warmth and instability. \n\nThe most notable impact through the period will be elevated \nwinds Friday and Saturday, initially out of the south and \nturning to the west Saturday after a strong cold front passes \nthrough Friday night. The most likely range for top wind gusts \nis 35-45 mph both days, though there is moderate confidence for\na few gusts to 50 mph Saturday in the Devils Lake Basin. Any \nexposed soils present a risk for patchy blowing dust during the \nwindiest periods. After temperatures well above seasonal \naverages for the next few days, a more typical northwest upper \nlevel flow returns high temperatures to the 40s and 50s starting\nSunday through at least the middle of next week. A mostly zonal\nflow does not favor any significant precipitation or \ntemperature anomalies next week.\n\nOverall, enjoy these last few days of dry weather and 60s to \n70s for highs, as they will become increasingly fleeting in the\ncoming weeks with seasonal normals dropping into the 30s and 40s\nfor highs.",
